          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Wear Layer Thickness Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose 12 mil for most living spaces and 20 mil for kitchens, entries, homes with large dogs and light commercial rooms; 6 to 8 mil is enough only for a guest bedroom or a closet. Wear layers above 20 mil are designed for retail floors and offices. Moving from 12 to 20 mil usually costs well under a dollar more per square foot, which makes it the cheapest durability upgrade available for a busy room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is total plank thickness a poor guide?</h3>

          <p>
            Boxes advertise overall thickness in millimeters, and that figure is largely marketing. A 7 mm plank and a 5 mm plank with the same top layer will wear at the same rate. The number that predicts scratch and scuff life is the wear layer, measured in mils, or thousandths of an inch. It is the clear urethane-and-vinyl skin laid over the printed image, and once foot traffic grinds through it, the print starts to show wear that only a new plank can fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How thick should the core be?</h3>

          <p>
            The core is the second number to check. Rigid stone-plastic composite cores resist dents and mask subfloor bumps better than flexible vinyl does. Read the core thickness apart from the total: an 8 mm plank that includes a 1.5 mm attached pad is really a 6.5 mm board. A thicker rigid core spans small dips more easily, but it is no excuse for a subfloor that is out of flat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do you need a plank with an attached pad?</h3>

          <p>
            An attached cork or IXPE foam pad adds comfort and sound control, and it generally means you should not lay a separate pad underneath. That saves buying underlayment on a wood subfloor, though a slab still calls for the vapor barrier the maker specifies.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which warranty terms actually matter?</h3>

          <p>
            Read the warranty wording, not the headline. A lifetime residential warranty often excludes scratches, dents, sun fading and water damage that comes up from below, so the fine print counts for more than the promise on the box. A 10- or 15-year light commercial warranty says more about how tough the surface really is than any lifetime residential claim.
          </p>

