        <div id="core-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is SPC or WPC Vinyl Plank Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            SPC is better where dents are the threat, and WPC is better where comfort and quiet matter more. Neither wins in every room, and flexible glue-down plank remains a third option for specific jobs. The differences come from what each core is made of.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is SPC vinyl plank?</h3>

          <p>
            SPC stands for stone plastic composite: powdered limestone blended with PVC and pressed into a dense board. It is heavy, hard and very dimensionally stable, so it stands up to rolling chairs and furniture legs better than other cores and barely moves when temperatures swing. The downside is the feel, which is firm and can sound clicky without a decent pad. SPC dominates the budget and mid-range market and is the default for most kitchens, basements and rental units.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is WPC vinyl plank?</h3>

          <p>
            WPC originally meant wood plastic composite, and today it describes a foamed PVC core that is thicker and lighter than SPC. It walks softer, warmer and quieter, which is why many premium lines are built on it. Because the core is foamed, it dents more easily under concentrated weight, such as a loaded piano bench, a refrigerator on narrow feet or a desk chair rolling without a mat. Felt pads and wide furniture cups keep that from becoming a real problem in most homes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does flexible glue-down plank make sense?</h3>

          <p>
            Flexible or glue-down plank is the older format. At 2 to 3 mm thick, it either glues to the subfloor or relies on a loose-lay backing, and it shows every bump beneath it, so it needs an almost perfect substrate. In return it handles commercial traffic, works on stairs and in rooms with large temperature swings, and can be replaced one plank at a time. Most homeowners installing their own floor will still do better with a click-lock rigid core.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do hybrid and mineral-core labels change anything?</h3>

          <p>
            Not much. Some brands now sell rigid cores containing mineral or magnesium and give them fresh names, but the questions stay the same: how well the plank resists dents under load, how thick the wear layer is, and how the locking joint survives years of expansion and contraction. Two products with the same wear layer and a similar weight per carton will usually perform alike, whatever their core is called.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pets and heavy furniture point to SPC; quiet and comfort point to WPC.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Start with SPC in any room that holds a refrigerator, a piano, rolling chairs or a large dog. In bedrooms and family rooms where people stand around and walk barefoot, WPC with attached cork feels clearly better.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="room-by-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Vinyl Plank Is Best for Each Room?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put SPC with a 20 mil wear layer in kitchens, save with 12 mil in bedrooms and living rooms, and let the vapor barrier and moisture test decide the basement. Each room adds one or two details of its own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Kitchens</h3>

          <p>
            A kitchen floor takes dropped pans, scraping chairs and standing water when a dishwasher leaks. Pick an SPC core with a wear layer of at least 20 mil and a tight angle-angle lock. Where the manufacturer allows it, run the floor into the dishwasher opening before the appliance goes in, and leave the specified gap at the cabinets. Never fasten cabinets down on top of a floating floor: that pins it in place and can make it buckle somewhere else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Basements</h3>

          <p>
            Basements are where vinyl plank built its reputation. It copes with a slightly damp concrete slab as long as you lay the vapor barrier the manufacturer requires. Test the slab first: tape down an 18-inch square of plastic for 24 to 48 hours, and if condensation forms underneath, you have a moisture problem to fix before any floor goes in. A <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier</a> that keeps the space below about 55 percent humidity protects the floor and everything stored on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bathrooms</h3>

          <p>
            The planks themselves will not swell, which makes vinyl a good fit for bathrooms. Seal the perimeter at the tub and toilet with flexible silicone so water cannot slip under the floor and sit on the subfloor. Most manufacturers do not allow the toilet flange to be bolted through the plank, so cut cleanly around the flange and caulk the joint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bedrooms and living rooms</h3>

          <p>
            These are the rooms to save money in. A 12 mil wear layer is plenty where traffic is light, and WPC or a cork-backed SPC gives a quieter, warmer floor. Sunrooms and rooms with big south-facing windows need a product rated for direct sun and the temperatures the room will reach. Look for a maximum temperature on the data sheet, since some rigid cores expand enough in strong sun to peak at the joints.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Radiant heat, unheated rooms and basement columns</h3>

          <p>
            Over radiant floor heating, make sure the plank is approved for it and keep the surface under the manufacturer's limit, which is often around 85 degrees Fahrenheit. Three-season rooms and unheated cabins need a product rated for unconditioned spaces; many are not, and putting one there can void the warranty. In a basement, check what stands on the slab, too. Rusty steel support columns and water heater stands should be treated before the new floor goes down around them, and our guide to <a href="/rust-converter-vs-rust-remover/">rust converter vs rust remover</a> explains which approach suits a column that cannot be moved.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subfloor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Flat Does a Subfloor Need to Be for Vinyl Plank?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Manufacturers typically call for a subfloor flat to within three-sixteenths of an inch over 10 feet, or an eighth of an inch over 6 feet. That tolerance matters because most failed vinyl plank floors fail at the joints, and most joint failures trace back to a subfloor that was not flat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you check flatness?</h3>

          <p>
            Lay a 6-foot level or a long aluminum straightedge across the room in several directions and slide a shim into any gap beneath it. Hollows deeper than the tolerance have to be filled, and humps have to be ground or sanded down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you fix concrete and wood subfloors?</h3>

          <p>
            On concrete, fill the low spots with a cement-based patch or self-leveling underlayment and take down high spots with a cup wheel on a grinder. On plywood or OSB, screw down loose panels to cure squeaks, sand any raised seams and fill dips with a patch rated for wood. A floating floor must never go over carpet, cushioned vinyl or anything else soft, because the locking joints flex with every footstep and eventually pull apart.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you install over old vinyl or tile?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually, if the old sheet vinyl or tile is firmly bonded and flat. Be careful, though: many floors laid before 1980, and the adhesives under them, contain asbestos. Do not sand, scrape or grind old resilient flooring until a sample has been checked for asbestos. Covering it with a floating floor is often the safest route, and most manufacturers permit it when the existing floor is a single, well-adhered layer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long should the planks acclimate?</h3>

          <p>
            Follow the carton instructions. Rigid cores need less time than wood, but most makers still want the boxes lying flat in the room at normal living temperature for 24 to 48 hours. Keep the house heated or cooled during the install and after it, because a floating floor laid in a cold, shut-up house will expand once the heat comes back on.
          </p>

