        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Install Click-Lock Vinyl Plank?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plan the layout, keep a quarter-inch gap at every edge, take the first two rows slowly and never force a joint. Click-lock vinyl plank is one of the friendliest floors for a first-time installer, and the steps below cover what decides whether it stays tight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What tools do you need?</h3>

          <p>
            The list is short: a utility knife with a good supply of blades, a speed square, a tapping block and pull bar made for the product, spacers, a rubber mallet and a jamb saw to undercut door casings. Rigid cores score and snap, so a saw is only needed for notches and rips, and a fine-tooth jigsaw or an oscillating tool handles both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you plan the layout?</h3>

          <p>
            Work out the layout before clicking a single plank. Divide the room width by the plank width so the final row is not a sliver, and if it would come out under about 2 inches, rip the first row narrower so both edges match. Stagger end joints by at least 6 to 8 inches, avoid repeating patterns, and draw planks from three or four cartons at a time so the color and grain variation spreads evenly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How big should the expansion gap be?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually about a quarter inch, all the way around, including at door jambs, pipes, cabinet toe kicks and stair noses. Baseboard or quarter-round hides it. Long continuous runs and big open plans often need a transition strip at doorways or every 40 to 50 feet. Check the maximum run length for your product, since going past it is one of the most common causes of peaking joints.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you lock the rows together?</h3>

          <p>
            Take your time on the first two rows, because every row after them follows their line. Engage the long side at a low angle, then drop the short end into place and tap it lightly with the block. Excess force cracks the tongue on a rigid core, and a cracked lock stays a weak spot for the life of the floor. When a joint refuses to close, unclick it and look for debris instead of hitting it harder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you finish the job?</h3>

          <p>
            Save the rooms with heavy appliances for last, and move appliances back across plywood or a hard runner so their feet do not gouge the new surface. Stick felt pads under every furniture leg before the furniture returns. You can walk on the floor the moment it is laid, one of its main advantages over glue-down products and site-finished wood.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Vinyl Plank Flooring Really Waterproof?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The plank is waterproof; the installed floor is not. On a vinyl plank box, waterproof means the plank will not swell or delaminate when water sits on it. It does not make the finished floor a pan.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does water get through?</h3>

          <p>
            Water that runs into the expansion gap at the walls or seeps between joints can reach the subfloor and stay there, sealed under a plastic floor that will not let it dry. On plywood, the result is swelling, odor and mold long before the floor itself looks damaged.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does the warranty cover?</h3>

          <p>
            Many warranties cover topical spills only. Flooding, hydrostatic pressure and water rising from below are usually excluded, so a plumbing leak or a seeping basement is your problem rather than the manufacturer's. Deal with spills promptly and seal the perimeter of wet rooms: in bathrooms and laundry rooms, run a bead of 100 percent silicone at the tub, shower and toilet, and along the walls as well if the manufacturer allows it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What happens after a flood?</h3>

          <p>
            Floating vinyl plank has one genuine advantage here. You can unclick the planks, pull them up, dry the subfloor with fans and a dehumidifier, and often reinstall the same planks. Keep a spare carton for that day. A wet-dry <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac</a> draws standing water out of the gap faster than towels can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you stop moisture from a slab?</h3>

          <p>
            On concrete, the vapor barrier is the defense against moisture rising from below. Use the poly sheet or barrier the manufacturer names, overlap the seams by 6 to 8 inches and tape them, and run the edges a little way up the wall behind the baseboard. A slab that is visibly damp, darkened in patches or white with efflorescence needs its water source fixed first, because no floor lasts long on a wet slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-laminate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Vinyl Plank Better Than Laminate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinyl plank is better wherever water is a real risk or the slab sits below grade; laminate is often better for scratch resistance in dry rooms. Many homes end up using both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does laminate win?</h3>

          <p>
            Laminate has improved a great deal. Current water-resistant lines with sealed edges and wax-treated cores put up with spills for many hours, and a melamine wear surface resists scratches better than most vinyl at the same price. It also feels closer to wood: harder, warmer in sound and more deeply embossed. In a dry living room or bedroom shared with pets, a good laminate is frequently the more scratch-resistant floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does vinyl plank win?</h3>

          <p>
            Vinyl's core never swells, however long water stands on it. It is also softer and quieter, and it forgives small subfloor flaws more readily. Basements, full bathrooms, laundry rooms, mudrooms and rentals usually lean toward vinyl. If you are torn between the two, our <a href="/best-laminate-flooring/">laminate flooring guide</a> explains the AC ratings and sealed-edge lines that narrow the gap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you use both in one house?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, and it often works best. Lay laminate through the bedrooms and living spaces and vinyl plank in the kitchen, bathrooms and basement. Matching colors across the two product types is easier than it once was, and a T-molding at the changeover is barely noticeable. Do not mix two floors within one open space, though, because small differences in sheen and thickness stand out in low, raking light.
          </p>

