        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Much Does Vinyl Plank Flooring Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, plank prices run from about $1.50 per square foot for budget rigid cores to around $7 for premium lines, and professional installation adds roughly $2 to $5 per square foot. Prices move with sales, so a large order is worth timing to a promotion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do the planks cost by tier?</h3>

          <p>
            Budget rigid-core planks begin around $1.50 to $2.50 per square foot. Mid-range lines with a 12 to 20 mil wear layer and an attached pad cost roughly $2.50 to $4. Premium brands with thick wear layers, cork backing and long commercial warranties land around $4 to $7.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What else goes in the budget?</h3>

          <p>
            Order 7 to 10 percent extra for waste in straightforward rooms and 12 to 15 percent for rooms with lots of corners, closets or a diagonal layout. Buy underlayment only when the plank has no attached pad, and allow for transition strips, quarter-round, a vapor barrier on concrete and floor patch. On a 400 square-foot room, those extras commonly add $200 to $500.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much does professional installation cost?</h3>

          <p>
            In most markets, a pro charges roughly $2 to $5 per square foot to install click-lock vinyl, and more when old flooring has to come out, the subfloor needs leveling or stairs are involved. Stairs are priced per step and take far longer to do well. A DIY install saves the most in large, open rooms with simple layouts and the least in small, broken-up spaces.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where should you spend and where should you save?</h3>

          <p>
            Spend on the wear layer and on subfloor prep. Save on total plank thickness, fancy bevel names and lifetime residential warranties that exclude almost everything. A 12 mil plank laid on a flat subfloor will beat a 22 mil plank laid over a wavy one every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Do You Clean and Repair Vinyl Plank Flooring?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Keep grit off the floor, damp-mop with a vinyl cleaner or plain warm water, and keep a spare carton for repairs. Grit is what wears through the wear layer, so most care comes down to stopping it at the door.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the right way to clean it?</h3>

          <p>
            Lay mats inside and outside every exterior door, and sweep or vacuum with a hard-floor head instead of a beater bar. Damp-mop with a cleaner made for vinyl or with plain warm water. Skip oil soaps, wax, paste polish and steam mops, which can leave a film or drive heat and moisture into the joints. Most manufacturers list the cleaners they approve in their care guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you prevent scratches and dents?</h3>

          <p>
            Felt pads under every chair and table leg do more than any cleaner, and they should be swapped out once they pick up grit. Put a clear chair mat under rolling office chairs, since hard casters will eventually scuff even a 20 mil surface. Under refrigerators and pianos, wide non-staining furniture cups spread the weight and keep softer cores from denting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about rugs and sunlight?</h3>

          <p>
            Rubber-backed rugs and mats can turn vinyl yellow over time through a chemical reaction. Choose mats labeled safe for vinyl floors, or a rug pad made of felt or natural rubber. In rooms with large windows, close the blinds on long sunny afternoons, because prolonged sun can fade some prints and warm the planks enough to make them expand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you replace a damaged plank?</h3>

          <p>
            Near a wall, the fix is simple: take off the baseboard, unclick the rows back to the damaged plank, swap it and click everything back together. In the middle of a room, installers cut the damaged plank out, trim the locking edges off the replacement and glue it into place. Either way, the spare carton saved from the install will match far better than a new box from another production run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Vinyl Plank Flooring
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on total thickness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overall plank thickness says little about wear: a thick plank with a thin wear layer scuffs as fast as a thin one. Rank options by wear layer in mils, then core type, then thickness. In kitchens, entries and big-dog homes, aim for 20 mil; the step up from 12 mil costs far less than replacing a worn floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the flatness check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A rigid core can hide a small bump, but it cannot hide a dip. When the subfloor sits outside the manufacturer's tolerance, the joints flex, the click locks wear and gaps open within a year or two. Check the room with a long straightedge, fill the low spots, grind the high ones, and only then lay the first plank.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pinning the floating floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Screwing kitchen islands, cabinets or heavy built-ins through a floating floor stops it from moving, and the stress surfaces as peaked joints somewhere else in the room. Set the cabinets first and run the floor up to them with a gap, or follow the manufacturer's rules for heavy fixed loads, so the whole floor stays free to expand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding a second pad under attached underlayment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Planks with attached IXPE or cork are built to sit straight on the subfloor or the vapor barrier. Slide a second foam layer underneath and the floor becomes too soft, flexing the locking joints with every step until they break. On a slab, use only a thin poly vapor barrier unless the instructions call for something else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting waterproof to protect the subfloor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A spill will not hurt the planks, but the plywood beneath them is another matter. Water that finds its way into the perimeter gap or a joint can sit there unseen for weeks. Caulk the edges of wet rooms with silicone, mop up spills quickly and repair leaks before they have a chance to soak the subfloor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which vinyl plank line suits most homes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A rigid-core plank carrying a 12 to 20 mil wear layer and an attached pad fits most homes. COREtec Plus Premium is the premium choice for comfort, Shaw Floorté Pro and Mohawk SolidTech Plus are tough mid-range options, and Lifeproof or NuCore deliver good value as long as you buy a mid-tier collection instead of the cheapest line.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is SPC or WPC better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the room. SPC is the denser core, so it shrugs off dents from appliances, furniture and rolling chairs and stays steadier through temperature swings. WPC is thicker, softer and quieter to walk on, which suits bedrooms and family rooms. Pick SPC for kitchens, basements and rentals, and WPC wherever comfort comes first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should the wear layer be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep 6 to 8 mil for closets and guest rooms, choose 12 mil for most bedrooms and living spaces, and step up to 20 mil for kitchens, entries, hallways and homes with large dogs. Wear layers above 20 mil target commercial spaces. Surface life follows the wear layer, not the overall plank thickness printed on the box.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you lay vinyl plank on a concrete slab?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and vinyl is one of the most forgiving floors for slabs, basements included. Run a moisture test first, fill dips and grind high spots until the slab meets the flatness tolerance, then lay the vapor barrier the maker specifies, usually 6 mil poly with taped seams. Fix the water problem on a visibly wet slab before any floor goes down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does vinyl plank dent or scratch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, it can do both. Heavy loads on narrow feet dent softer cores, and grit dragged underfoot scratches the wear layer. Put wide coasters under appliances and pianos, felt pads under every chair leg, a mat beneath rolling chairs and walk-off mats at each door. A 20 mil wear layer on an SPC core resists both kinds of damage best.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put vinyl plank in a bathroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Water will not swell the planks, so vinyl makes a sound bathroom floor. Keep the expansion gap, seal the perimeter at the tub, shower and toilet with 100 percent silicone, and skip bolting the toilet through the floor where the manufacturer forbids it. Wipe up standing water promptly so none of it reaches the subfloor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does vinyl plank flooring last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Installed over a flat, dry subfloor, a quality plank commonly lasts 15 to 25 years in a home, and longer in quiet rooms. Thin wear layers in busy kitchens and entries give out sooner, sometimes within 5 to 10 years. Doormats, felt pads and gentle cleaning add considerably to the life of any vinyl floor.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

